{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they stood before Moshe, etc.</b> In parashat Beha'alotekha (Numbers 9:6) I explained the dispute between Abba Ḥanan and Rabbi Yoshiyah, and also why they disagree in this place as well; and according to what I explained there, the verse's saying 'all the princes and all the congregation' will also be resolved according to both reasoning: for the one who says 'one divides honor,' etc. — from here you learn that even if one's teacher does not divide honor for him, the princes do not divide honor for a commoner; and for the one who says 'one does not divide,' etc. — from here you are forced to emend the verse and interpret it accordingly, and understand. And the reason they spoke at a time when the tribes of Israel were gathered together is to state their words before all of them, so that they would know his reply to them and not raise objections after their entry into the land." "<b>And in the book</b> of the holy Zohar, parashat Balak, page 207a, they said, and this is their language: 'They were united in the wilderness in entreaty before Moshe, because they considered Moshe the advocate of their father, and because of this they approached before Moshe and Elazar and all the princes and all the heads of the fathers,' etc., end quote. These words indicate that he explains the reason for the mention of 'the princes and all the congregation' — namely, that the daughters brought them in out of concern that Moshe might bear them hostility on account of their father. And what they said in the Zohar, 'and all the heads of the fathers,' is an explanation of the verse's saying 'and all the congregation'; and one is compelled to explain thus, for according to what he explains — that they brought them in — the entire congregation would not assemble except by a decree of Moshe and a blast of the trumpets, as is known; therefore he explained that they were the heads of the fathers." "And there is reason to note regarding the words of the Zohar: how could it enter the mind of these righteous women to harbor suspicion against one as worthy as Moshe? Perhaps it is only to teach us the way of life, as he explained there in the Zohar, and this is his language: 'From here, whoever suspects a judge should bring others along and increase men,' end quote. And the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, already stated (Bava Batra 119a) that the daughters were wise and righteous, and they certainly knew that Moshe would never pervert justice. Or perhaps they feared that Moshe would judge them by the standard of the complainers and the spies, whose children did not receive a portion in the land because they spoke against HaShem and against Moshe; therefore they assembled all those mentioned, to support them in the judgment so that he would not incline in his mind against them. Or perhaps they were clever in this so that Moshe would feel it and do what he did, as stated there in the Zohar, that he withdrew from the judgment and brought it before HaShem. And it appears that even though Moshe knew that they were to receive an inheritance, nevertheless he did so to teach judges that when they see they are suspected in judgment, they should withdraw, etc., in the manner that he did. Or perhaps he was concerned for them lest they be like the complainers, and he removed himself from supporting their judgment and brought it before HaShem."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:13.782683999Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}